CONSULT-63 — Version-strategy angle (Miles 7.2a/7.2e vs 9.3b/9.3v)
====================================================================
Consultant: fresh Sonnet 5, read-only mechanism/feasibility analysis. No code touched.

Sources read:
- D:\Code\milesss-v9.3b\README.md (SIGMA/Archivo Federal preservation repo, MSS 9.3b w/ source)
- D:\Code\milesss-v9.3b\win\sdk\help\Miles.chm, decompiled via `hh.exe -decompile` (Windows'
  built-in CHM decompiler) into changelog.html (full version history back to MSS 3.6, 1997),
  migration.html, AIL_register_stream_callback.html, AIL_stream_status.html, and the
  "glitch when looping MP3" FAQ.
- src/external/3rd/library/miles-7.2e/include/mss.h (6693 lines) vs
  src/external/3rd/library/miles-9.3b/include/mss.h (8352 lines) — full symbol/enum diff.
- src/engine/client/library/clientAudio/src/win32/{Audio,Sound2d,Sound3d,Sample2d,Sample3d,
  SampleStream}.cpp/.h — every AIL_* callsite.
- clientAudio.vcxproj and SwgClient.vcxproj (Win32/x64, all 5 configs) — include/lib wiring.
- git show 984afc073 (the 9.3b→9.3v x64 swap commit).

====================================================================
1. DOCUMENTED CHANGES 7.x → 9.3 (streams/EOS/MP3/mixer/file-IO/buffering)
====================================================================

The changelog.html I extracted is the REAL, dated RAD changelog (not the SIGMA fork's marketing
text) — 1997 through 2012-12-12 (9.3b). Two entries are architecture-level rewrites of exactly
the subsystem implicated in the crackle-storm bug, and they land squarely BETWEEN 7.2e (our
rollback candidate, 2008-10-30) and 9.3b (what we ship):

  A) "Changes from 8.0f to 8.0g (12-06-2010)":
     "All new IO and streaming system (optimized with Telemetry) - uses background thread
     loading on all platforms (including Wii). Much, much better sharing when reading from
     multiple streams at once. You can even read static files during streaming safely."
     Also added in this release: AIL_stream_filled_percent, AIL_timer_thread_handle,
     AIL_IO_thread_handle. This is a confirmed, RAD-labeled REWRITE of the stream/IO layer,
     not an incremental patch. Every stream-open/EOF/prime code path our engine drives
     (AIL_open_stream → AIL_register_stream_callback → AIL_start_stream, polled via
     AIL_stream_status) runs through this rewritten engine in 9.3b; it did NOT exist yet in
     7.2e. This is the single strongest documented reason the two versions could differ in
     EOS-callback behavior at exactly a stream-transition boundary.

  B) "Changes from 9.1f to 9.2a (7-12-2012)": "Default mix granularity is now 1 millisecond
     on all platforms." — CONFIRMED BY HEADER DIFF (below): this is a real, dated, global
     change to the DirectSound mixing tick rate, landing AFTER 7.2e and folded into the 9.3b
     we ship.

  Other documented items touching the exact call surface in use here:
  - 7.2d→7.2e (10-30-2008, i.e. THIS version): "Made AIL_decompress_ASI not take Miles mixer
    lock on modern platforms (prevents skipping when decoding heavy duty files while sound is
    playing)." — so even 7.2e itself already carries an ASI-decode/mixer-lock decoupling fix;
    rolling back to 7.2e does NOT roll back past this specific fix.
  - 8.0c→8.0d (5-17-2010): "Clarified the documentation for the DIG_3D_MUTE_AT_MAX preference
    and related behavior." — this preference's default value literally flips between our two
    candidate versions (see 2.3 below); the 8.0d note is RAD's own acknowledgment that this
    preference's behavior needed clarification in this exact version window.
  - 8.0d→8.0e (7-7-2010): "Fixed a bug that could cause audible glitches after multiple
    iterations of looped .MP3s and other compressed samples." — a DIRECT MP3-looping-glitch
    fix that postdates 7.2e. Not our exact symptom (ours is one-shots at a *stream transition*,
    not looped-MP3 iteration), but it establishes that MP3-loop/iteration glitches were a live,
    version-specific defect class in this exact era, fixed AFTER the version we're
    contemplating rolling back to.
  - 8.0i→8.0j (1-26-2011): "Fixed bug that could cause AIL_stream_status() to incorrectly
    return SMP_PLAYING after stream termination." + "Fixed bug that could cause
    AIL_start_stream() to fail to restart a completed stream under certain conditions." —
    stream-status/restart correctness bugs, fixed between 7.2e and 9.3b. Rolling back to 7.2e
    would resurrect these (already-fixed) bugs as a cost of the rollback, which matters because
    our engine's own EOF detection is a poll of AIL_stream_status()==SMP_DONE — i.e. we lean
    directly on the correctness of the exact function RAD had to patch post-7.2e.
  - 9.0b→9.0c (7-28-2011): "Fix AIL_stream_status for very short sound samples." — another
    stream-status fix in this window.
  - 6.6d→6.6e (2004, ancient but instructive): "Changed some logic to allow streams to be
    closed inside an end of stream callback" — shows EOS-callback/stream-lifetime interactions
    have historically been a recurring fragile corner of this exact API, going back to 2004.

  9.3-sub-version deltas (9.3a/9.3b/9.3g/9.3v) found in the changelog: 9.3a (11-26-2012, "big
  new release") added push/pop system state, sample groups, scheduled starts, 1ms-granularity-
  everywhere confirmation, spread/line-segment positioning. 9.3b (12-12-2012) is WAV marker/loop
  and Bink-only fixes — nothing stream/EOS/MP3-related changed 9.3a→9.3b. There is no public
  changelog entry for "9.3g" or "9.3v" (these appear to be post-retail/internal RAD builds not
  covered by the archived SDK's own changelog — consistent with 9.3v being "the fixed later RAD
  build" per commit 984afc073, i.e. a hotfix branch off 9.3-family that never got a public
  changelog entry in this archive).

  Net read: nothing in the public changelog specifically documents "the stream EOS callback
  stops firing for MP3 streams" as a fix or regression at any version boundary. But the two
  architecture rewrites (8.0g IO/streaming, 9.2a mixer granularity) are exactly the kind of
  wholesale internal replacement that would silently change EOS-callback delivery timing/
  reliability without RAD ever writing a changelog line about "streaming/EOS callback: this now
  behaves differently," because from RAD's perspective it's an internal implementation detail,
  not an API contract change.

====================================================================
2. 7.2e ROLLBACK FEASIBILITY (Win32 client only)
====================================================================

2.1 Header/API surface diff — CLEAN.
Extracted every AIL_* symbol used across Audio.cpp/Sound2d.cpp/Sound3d.cpp/Sample2d.cpp/
Sample3d.cpp/SampleStream.cpp (77 unique symbols) and grepped each against
miles-7.2e/include/mss.h. Zero real misses. The two apparent misses were false positives:
"AIL_file_" (a substring from a comment, "AIL_file_*_callback ABI", in Audio.cpp:318) and
"AIL_stream_volume_levels" (a DEAD, commented-out line at Audio.cpp:3566 — this identifier
doesn't even exist in the 9.3b header we actually build against, confirming it's inert).
Every preference enum used (DIG_3D_MUTE_AT_MAX, DIG_DS_FRAGMENT_CNT, DIG_MIXER_CHANNELS,
DIG_DS_MIX_FRAGMENT_CNT, SMP_DONE/PLAYING/STOPPED/FREE, MSS_MC_* speaker specs,
MSS_STREAM_CHUNKS) is present in 7.2e too. The file-callback typedefs (AIL_file_open_callback/
_close_callback/_seek_callback/_read_callback, and AIL_set_file_callbacks's signature) are
BYTE-FOR-BYTE identical in shape between 7.2e and 9.3b (U32 return, UINTa handle) — the
TreeFile file-I/O redirection would compile and link unchanged. clientAudio only
`#include <mss.h>` (FirstClientAudio.h:18); 7.2e's mss.h is self-contained (predates the
rrcore.h basic-type unification that landed at 8.0d→8.0e, so it doesn't need or want rrcore.h
alongside it — and clientAudio never includes rrcore.h itself).

  Verdict: a straight recompile of clientAudio (and anything else linking Mss32.lib) against
  7.2e would very likely compile clean with ZERO source changes. This is a meaningfully
  stronger result than "probably fine" — it's a verified zero-diff API surface over the
  specific ~77 symbols this engine actually touches.

2.2 Build-graph work-list (Win32 only; x64 stays untouched).
  - clientAudio.vcxproj: 3 Win32 ItemDefinitionGroups (Debug/Optimized/Release) each have one
    `AdditionalIncludeDirectories` line with `...\miles-9.3b\include` — repoint to
    `...\miles-7.2e\include`. (x64 Debug/Release configs keep miles-9.3b\include — untouched.)
  - SwgClient.vcxproj: 5 occurrences of `miles-9.3b\lib` in AdditionalLibraryDirectories (one
    per config: Debug/Optimized/Release Win32 + Debug/Release x64). Repoint the 3 Win32 ones to
    `miles-7.2e\lib\win`. AdditionalDependencies already references `Mss32.lib` (capital M) —
    that's the EXACT filename 7.2e ships (`lib\win\Mss32.lib`), so no rename needed; the 9.3b
    lib happens to be lowercase `mss32.lib` but Windows link.exe resolution is
    case-insensitive, so this was never a hazard either way.
  - Deploy: 7.2e's redist/ dir is a complete, matched set — mss32.dll, mssmp3.asi (the MP3
    ASI decoder itself — the component most under suspicion), mssogg.asi, mssvoice.asi, and
    the dolby/ds3d/dsp/eax/srs .flt filters + Msseax.m3d. This is everything currently staged
    under stage/miles/ for 9.3b, so it's a pure folder-swap for an A/B build — no partial/
    mixed-vintage redist risk (the exact trap the 9.3b→9.3v x64 swap commit had to work around
    with a "matched set" comment).
  - Any OTHER project besides clientAudio/SwgClient that includes mss.h or links Mss32.lib
    would also need the same include/lib repoint (not audited beyond clientAudio+SwgClient
    here — a `grep -rl "miles-9.3b"` across the full src tree before doing this for real is a
    5-minute sanity check, cheap insurance).

2.3 Behavioral risk even after a clean compile — the DIG_3D_MUTE_AT_MAX case study.
This is the concrete, already-discovered example of "compiles fine, silently changes behavior"
that the task asked me to find, and it is a textbook one:

    7.2e:  #define DIG_3D_MUTE_AT_MAX  46      DEFAULT_D3MAM = NO   ("off by default")
    9.3b:  #define DIG_3D_MUTE_AT_MAX  11      DEFAULT_D3MAM = YES  ("on by default")

  Two things changed, not one: (a) the DEFAULT flipped NO→YES between these versions
  (documented in-header, and independently corroborated by the 8.0c→8.0d changelog line about
  clarifying this exact preference), and (b) the preference's numeric ID itself was
  renumbered (46 → 11) — Miles preference IDs are NOT ABI-stable across major version
  boundaries. Audio.cpp:99-101,1513-1516 already contains a hand-written comment and explicit
  `AIL_set_preference(DIG_3D_MUTE_AT_MAX, 0)` fix for exactly this default-flip, dated to a
  prior investigation (comment says "default YES in 9.3x"). A 7.2e rollback would silently
  re-enable a NO-OP fix (setting an already-NO-by-default preference to 0 again is harmless),
  but it's proof this exact hazard class (recompile-clean, behavior-different) is real and has
  already bitten this codebase once. The renumbering point is the sharper risk: it means the
  header and the runtime DLL must always be from the SAME matched version pair, or any raw/
  cached preference ID would silently target the wrong preference. This is the same
  "shared-header ABI trap" pattern already in this project's memory bank, just recurring at
  the Miles-preference-enum layer instead of the C++-struct layer.

2.4 The bigger, quantified behavioral delta: mixer granularity and buffer sizing.
Diffing the two headers' Windows-specific preference-default blocks (both headers, guarded
under `#if defined(IS_WINDOWS)`) gives hard numbers, not just prose:

    7.2e  (Win32 DirectSound block, mss.h:1564-1573):
      DIG_DS_FRAGMENT_SIZE default = 8 ms
      DIG_DS_FRAGMENT_CNT  default = 96        → ring = 8ms x 96  = 768 ms
      DIG_DS_MIX_FRAGMENT_CNT default = 8 fragments → mix-ahead = 8ms x 8 = 64 ms

    9.3b  (mss.h:781-791, comments are RAD's own):
      DIG_DS_FRAGMENT_SIZE default = 1 ms      // "1 ms per mix."
      DIG_DS_FRAGMENT_CNT  default = 256       // "We want ~256 ms of buffers"
      DIG_DS_MIX_FRAGMENT_CNT default = 48     // "Mix ahead ~48 ms"

  This is the header-level confirmation of the changelog's "default mix granularity is now
  1 millisecond on all platforms" (9.1f→9.2a) line, with actual numbers: the Windows mixer
  granularity moved from 8ms ticks / 768ms ring / 64ms mix-ahead (7.2e) to 1ms ticks / 256ms
  ring / 48ms mix-ahead (9.3b) — an 8x finer tick rate on a ring buffer 3x smaller. This lines
  up suspiciously well with the evidence pack's measured "OPENCALL 16ms after the EOF poll
  fired" and the engine's own in-game mix-ahead override (16ms, i.e. BELOW even 9.3b's already-
  tightened 48ms stock default) — the whole system is now operating at a timing granularity an
  order of magnitude finer than what 7.2e's internals were designed around. AIL_MM_PERIOD's
  stock default also moved (7.2e Windows: 5ms service tick; 9.3b: DEFAULT_AMP=1, i.e. ~1ms,
  though the in-header comment text is stale and still says "5 msec" — the VALUE is what the
  code reads, and it's 1). None of this proves the storm is a 7.2e-vs-9.3b regression, but it
  is hard, header-sourced evidence that the two versions' internal mixing cadence differs by
  close to an order of magnitude on exactly the platform (Windows/DirectSound) and exactly the
  parameter class (fragment/ring/mix-ahead timing) implicated by the symptom's own ~16ms
  measured latency.

2.5 Overall rollback verdict.
NOT impractical. This is a low-effort, low-risk, high-information experiment for the Win32
client specifically: 3 include-path edits + 3 lib-path edits + a redist folder swap, zero
source changes predicted (verified against the actual 77-symbol usage surface), and the 7.2e
SDK is already fully vendored in-repo (headers + Mss32.lib + a complete matched redist,
including the mssmp3.asi decoder itself). The engineering cost is a rebuild + an A/B repro run,
not a source-code project. The main real risk is unknown-unknowns in the ~8300-vs-6700-line
header body outside the 77 symbols actually exercised (e.g. struct layout for AILSOUNDINFO or
similar structs passed by value/pointer, if any are used) — worth one targeted check
(`grep -n "AILSOUNDINFO\|MSS_" ` across clientAudio) before committing to a real build, but
nothing found in this pass suggests a blocking incompatibility.

====================================================================
3. x64 DISCRIMINATION MATRIX (running the repro on mss64.dll 9.3v)
====================================================================

3.1 What the test tells you, per outcome.

  STORM REPRODUCES on x64 (9.3v):
    Strongest reading: the defect is NOT a narrow property of the specific mss32.dll 9.3b
    binary. It's shared across at least two members of the "modern Miles" family (9.3b x86,
    9.3v x64) that differ in bitness, in the specific 3D-mixer fix, and (per the mismatched
    dolby/dsp/srs .flt FileVersion 9.3g vs the core mss64.dll's 9.3v) probably in patch
    provenance too. Given they share almost nothing except "post-8.0g rewritten IO/streaming
    core, 1ms mixer granularity, our engine's exact call pattern," reproducing on x64 pushes
    weight AWAY from "one specific binary's bug" and onto either (a) something structural in
    the 9.x-family stream/EOS design common to both runtimes, or (b) an engine-side usage
    pattern that would reproduce against ANY sufficiently-modern Miles runtime regardless of
    vendor patch level. This test alone cannot distinguish (a) from (b) — but it does rule out
    "just a 9.3b-Win32-build quirk," which is valuable.

  STORM DOES NOT REPRODUCE on x64 (9.3v):
    Weaker/more ambiguous reading, for two reasons: (1) the repro is timing-precise (trigger
    within roughly 1s of a track transition, specifically overlapping the ~16ms
    EOF-poll→OPENCALL window) — a single clean run on a differently-scheduled binary is weak
    evidence of true absence; it needs several repeated attempts at the exact same
    "door-at-theme-end" timing before "no storm" is trustworthy. (2) Even if genuinely absent,
    you cannot yet say WHY without further isolation (see 3.3) — candidates are "9.3v fixed it"
    (a specific claim, testable per 3.3), "64-bit code path/SIMD/ASM differences avoid the
    race" (bitness, not version), or "x64 client's overall frame pacing/thread scheduling
    shifts the race window enough to dodge it" (an engine/OS-scheduling confound, not a Miles
    fix at all).

3.2 Confounds that limit inference from a bare x64 run.
  - BITNESS is entangled with the version swap: mss64.dll 9.3v is compiled from different
    platform-specific code paths (own SIMD/ASM mixer, per the 8.0a-era changelog note "Merged
    the Win32 and Win64 builds" only in the *build system*, not the machine code) than
    mss32.dll 9.3b. A pure x64-vs-x86 test can't separate "fixed by 9.3v's patches" from
    "never was broken in the 64-bit compiled mixer to begin with."
  - THE 9.3v RUNTIME IS ALREADY A CHIMERA, not a clean single version: mss64.dll itself
    reports 9.3v, but its own bundled filter DLLs (dolby/dsp/srs .flt) report FileVersion
    9.3g — a different sub-version than the core. Any x64 result is a property of this mixed
    bundle, not of "9.3v" as a monolithic, well-defined version. There is no public changelog
    entry in the archived SDK for either "9.3g" or "9.3v," consistent with both being
    post-retail/internal RAD hotfix builds outside this changelog's coverage — meaning we
    can't cross-reference *what* specifically 9.3v changed relative to 9.3b from documentation
    alone; only empirical A/B (3.3) can tell you.
  - THE ONE-SHOT TRIGGER SOUND ITSELF MAY RIDE THE KNOWN-DIFFERENT 3D-MIXER PATH. This is the
    sharpest, least-obvious confound: the crisp repro trigger is explicitly "the cantina-door
    sound," and commit 984afc073 (the 9.3b→9.3v x64 adoption) names "cantina door" verbatim as
    one of the 3D-positional sounds that was BROKEN on 9.3b-x64 and FIXED by 9.3v. If that
    door sound is played through the engine's Sound3d/Sample3d path (3D-positioned one-shot,
    which is the normal way ambient/interactive world sounds are authored in this engine, as
    opposed to Sound2d/Sample2d for UI), then an x64 storm test with the door trigger is not a
    clean "same interaction, different runtime" comparison — it's running the MP3-stream-
    transition race THROUGH a 3D-mixer code path that 9.3v specifically patches relative to
    9.3b-x64, on a platform (x64) where that patch is known to change audible behavior for
    this exact sound. A "clean" outcome on x64 could reflect the 3D-mixer fix incidentally
    altering (masking OR revealing) the crackle rather than telling you anything about the
    stream-EOS/transition defect per se. Recommend confirming whether the door SFX is 2D or
    3D before trusting the x64 result, and/or repeating the x64 test with a known-2D one-shot
    (a UI blip, or any Sound2d-authored SFX) alongside the door, to separate "MP3-transition
    race" from "3D-mixer interaction" as two independently-varying axes.
  - PROVIDER/PATH SELECTION: confirm getCurrent3dProvider()/getProviderSpec() resolve to the
    same digital-driver provider on both builds before treating the two runs as
    apples-to-apples; if x64 defaults to a different provider (e.g. because 9.3v's redist
    ships/registers filters differently), that's yet another uncontrolled variable.
  - x64 CLIENT FRAME PACING/SCHEDULING: this is a fundamentally different, freshly-landed
    64-bit binary (Phase 33/34 milestone) with its own thread scheduling, GC-equivalent
    behavior, and frame timing — any of which could shift whether the ~16ms race window is hit
    at all during a given transition, independent of anything Miles-side.

3.3 A sharper, available third test: isolate "9.3b vs 9.3v" from "32-bit vs 64-bit" directly.
Unlike the header/lib situation, this is a pure DLL swap with NO recompile: miles-9.3b itself
ships a full matched redist64 (src/external/3rd/library/miles-9.3b/redist64/, including
mss64.dll AND mss64mp3.asi — the x64 MP3 decoder). Better still, there is already a backup of
the pre-swap 9.3b x64 binary sitting in the tree: stage-x64/_miles93b_bak/mss64.dll (vs the
live stage-x64/mss64.dll, which is 9.3v). Swapping mss64.dll (and its matched mss64mp3.asi,
to avoid a mixed-vintage core/codec pairing) back to the 9.3b redist64 set, on the SAME x64
SwgClient_r/d.exe, holding bitness constant, isolates the 9.3b-vs-9.3v variable cleanly — no
rebuild required, just a file swap + repro run. This is a stronger, one-variable-at-a-time
experiment than "run the shipped x64 client" and should be preferred if Kenny wants a real
discriminating result rather than a single ambiguous data point. Caveat: this reintroduces
the KNOWN 9.3b-x64 3D-mixer bug (silent/spotty 3D positional audio) for the duration of the
test — irrelevant to the crackle-storm mechanism itself (which is 2D MP3 stream timing) unless
the trigger SFX is 3D-positioned (see 3.2's door-sound confound), in which case that known
breakage could itself mask whether the crackle occurs.

3.4 Recommended sequencing.
  1. Confirm whether the repro's one-shot trigger (cantina door) is authored 2D or 3D.
  2. Run the repro as-is on the shipped x64 client (9.3v) — cheapest, zero-setup data point.
  3. Whatever the outcome, swap in the 9.3b redist64 (mss64.dll + mss64mp3.asi from
     miles-9.3b/redist64/, using the existing _miles93b_bak/mss64.dll as the known-good
     backup source) on the same x64 exe and repeat, to separate "9.3b vs 9.3v" from
     "32-bit vs 64-bit" cleanly.
  4. If practical, repeat both x64 runs with a 2D-authored one-shot substituted for the door,
     to separate "MP3-transition race" from "3D-mixer interaction."
  Four data points (Win32 9.3b / x64 9.3v / x64 9.3b / x64+2D-trigger) will localize the
  defect far more precisely than any single x64 run, at a cost of a few file swaps and repro
  passes — no rebuild needed for any of them.
